er success note and traffic summary, 4 Nov (document): On 30 Oct Orrin Media began migrating its archive into Tarnfield, writing about 18 TB/day across all three regions. It is a background copy with a 15 Dec deadline and is currently ahead of schedule. Orrin's contract allows temporary ingest rate limits with 24 hours' notice, and their technical lead has said pacing is fine. Fleet traffic, 28 Oct to 4 Nov: GET requests/s rose 6%, gradually across the week. PUT requests/s rose 14%.\n\nBRIEF:\nDon't roll back the gateway. The latency jump comes from the storage compaction tuning pushed on the evening of 2 Nov. Compaction threads went from 2 to 6, disks went to about 97% busy within 5 minutes in every cell, and node-side read time rose from 61 to 262 ms. Per cell, p99 rose minutes after that push, not when each cell's gateway upgrade arrived. For example, use-1 got 5.8 at 22:05 but only degraded at 23:40, right after the push. The cell that skipped the push (aps-3) got 5.8 and stayed at about 94 ms. Rolling euw-1 back to 5.7 changed nothing. Network and traffic growth don't explain the pattern. Rolling back would reopen the security hole and wouldn't fix latency. Next step: revert the compaction tuning in one cell, watch p99 and backlog, then extend it if it works. Backlog is about 4% and projected to stay under 15% until at least 13 Nov, so there is room to do this carefully. Do not pause compaction fleet-wide. Hold off on rate-limiting Orrin until the 10 Nov backlog attribution is in. Confidence is high on the cause and lower on what drove the backlog growth.\n","numLines":20,"startLine":1,"totalLines":20}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012jfkxpLzueNvdRXY8KWtMS","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
